On Wed, Nov 9, 2011 at 1:07 PM, djandus <jandew@gmail.com> wrote:

> So, everyone knows that the most important quality of any language is its
> ability to tell jokes. (sarcasm)
>
> Regardless, in trying to translate a classic lightbulb joke, the closest I
> got was:
> ni**tcu xo lo prenu ku lo pu'u zbasu se carna lo cukpulterg**u'i
>
> Basically, how do you describe "screwing in" a lightbulb, and how do you
> say "lightbulb"? In my example, I went with a sort of
> "putting-together/installing kind-of turning" for "screwing in", and "round
> protrusion light-source" for "lightbulb". (I figured out the latter based
> on suggestions from the plant bulb.)
>
> If this is the actual good way of doing this, then I guess {tolzbasu
> carna} would be for "unscrewing"? Is this something worth making a lujvo
> for, what with using an actual screwdriver? (How would you say
> "screwdriver," even? {carna selpilno}?)
>

  A screwdriver is a lupcartci

  If you want to "change" a lightbulb, that would be "basygau" but if you
just want to "unscrew" it, that would be "vimcu" or "tolse'a".  On the
other hand, if you really want to emphasize the rotational nature, I'd
suggest "carvi'u"

        --gejyspa

